s=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">clogpow</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hle</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">suffices</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">clog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">nextPowerOfTwo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">clog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nextPow2_nat_le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">clog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">le_pow_clog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">decide</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">hk</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">apply_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">clog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">using</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">clog_monotone</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simpa</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">clogpow</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">using</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><em>Notes on AI usage: I used Claude Code to adapt the (previously human-written) proofs such that they no longer need Mathlib tactics.</em></p>",
        "id": 611309278,
        "sender_full_name": "Adomas Baliuka",
        "timestamp": 1784300229
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you for suggesting these lemmas. I'm adding the ones from your first snippet to core in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14458\">lean4#14458</a>, though I chose a slighly different proof strategy, deducing them as corollaries of a single lemma that exactly characterizes <code>n.nextPowerOfTwo</code>.</p>",
        "id": 611620609,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1784538174
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks!</p>\n<p>Can you maybe also comment on the general question, that is, what should I have done, ideally?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maybe posting like this was best already? </li>\n<li>Or make an RFC to core?</li>\n<li>Or PR to core directly since it's just very few lemmas which are already written (easy to just close PR if not desired)? (Asking this since there seem to be a lot of sich small PRs that do not actually have an RFC issue, even though the instructions say one should have one.)</li>\n<li>Make a PR to Batteries or Mathlib instead?</li>\n<li>Mostly avoid bothering people about such lemmas?</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 611741774,
        "sender_full_name": "Adomas Baliuka",
        "timestamp": 1784574850
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Unfortunately there isn't a simple rule. For core, it's generally a good idea to only open a PR if you have explicit confirmation from a maintainer that a PR is welcome (there are exceptions. If you're just fixing a typo in a docstring, it's probably fine, and if you have a long history with the project then things are different of course). However, for the standard library team it is very useful to know that people stumbled over a gap in the library (we generally know about the holes, but we don't always know how to prioritze them). So a message on Zulip or a bug report or RFC is appreciated, and sometimes we will then indicate that a PR is welcome.</p>\n<p>Batteries or mathlib are much more open to PRs, and the standard library team regularly looks at them for material that is suitable for inclusion in core, so that path is an option, but keep in mind that Batteries and mathlib (and cslib) have their own ideas of what is in scope for them.</p>",
        "id": 611816656,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1784615746
    }
]